GAD.reg("lesson","fm.ratio.profit",{"id":"fm.ratio.profit","title":"Profitability and market ratios, and the DuPont analysis","md":"## In plain words\n\n### Same twelve and a half per cent, different sums\n\nKesar has just read BrewBucks India's annual report.\n\n> **Kesar:** BrewBucks India earns 12.5% on its shareholders' money. Our café company earns 12.5% too. Dev, we are as good as the Americans. Well done!\n> **Dev:** Present value of that compliment: lower than you think, Kesar-ji. Same answer, not necessarily the same sums.\n> **Kesar:** Twelve and a half is twelve and a half.\n> **Dev:** Not when one of the three engines behind it runs on borrowed money, and borrowed money sends a bill every month.\n\nProfit by itself says little: ₹90 crore is a fortune for a tea stall and a disaster for a bank. **Profitability ratios** compare profit with something, and ICAI groups them by that something:\n\n- with **sales**: how many rupees of each ₹100 of sales remain as gross profit, operating profit or net profit (the **margins**);\n- with the **investment**: the return on assets, on capital employed and on the shareholders' equity;\n- per **share**: earnings and dividend per share, the owner's view;\n- with the **market price** of the share: the price-earnings ratio and the yields, the investor's view.\n\nTapri's year to 31 March 2026: sales ₹900 crore, profit after tax ₹90 crore, a **net profit ratio** of 10%. The equity shareholders' funds are ₹300 crore and they earned ₹88 crore after the preference dividend, a **return on equity** of 29.3%. With 4 crore shares, that is ₹22 a share (**EPS**), and at a market price of ₹330 the share sells at 15 times its earnings (the **P/E ratio**).\n\nDev's three engines are the **DuPont analysis**: return on equity is the product of three ratios, the margin on sales, the sales produced per rupee of assets, and the assets carried per rupee of equity. The last one grows with debt, so it shows how much of the return is borrowed.\n\n::: analogy Three ways to fill the glass\nA chaiwala can earn more on his own money in three ways: keep more per cup (margin), sell more cups from the same stall (turnover), or set up a bigger stall with a moneylender's cash (the equity multiplier). The first two are skill. The third works only while each cup earns more than the moneylender charges, and the moneylender comes every month either way.\n:::\n\n::: key\nProfitability ratios relate profit to sales (margins), to the money invested (ROA, ROCE, ROE), to each share (EPS, DPS) and to the share's price (P/E, yields, market-to-book). Return on investment = margin × turnover, and return on equity = net profit margin × asset turnover × equity multiplier: two companies with the same ROE can be very different businesses.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### Four groups\n\nThe profitability ratios measure the profitability or the operational efficiency of the firm. They reflect the final results of business operations; they are some of the most closely watched and widely quoted ratios, and management attempts to maximise them to maximise the firm's value (SM 3.18). ICAI's four groups (SM 3.19):\n\n| Group | Ratios |\n|---|---|\n| (i) Based on sales | Gross profit ratio; net profit ratio; operating profit ratio; expenses ratios |\n| (ii) Overall return on assets / investments | Return on investment (ROI): return on assets (ROA), return on capital employed (ROCE), return on equity (ROE) |\n| (iii) From the owner's point of view | Earnings per share (EPS); dividend per share (DPS); dividend pay-out ratio |\n| (iv) Market / valuation / investors | Price-earnings ratio; dividend and earnings yield; market value to book value per share; Q ratio |\n\n### Margins: every line of the profit statement over sales\n\nTapri's statement of profit for 2025-26, each line as a percentage of sales:\n\n| Particulars | ₹ crore | % of sales | Ratio |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Sales | 900 | 100.00 | |\n| Less: Cost of goods sold | 540 | 60.00 | COGS ratio |\n| **Gross profit** | **360** | **40.00** | Gross profit ratio |\n| Less: Administrative expenses | 100 | 11.11 | |\n| Less: Selling and distribution expenses | 128 | 14.22 | |\n| **Operating profit (EBIT)** | **132** | **14.67** | Operating profit ratio |\n| Less: Interest | 12 | 1.33 | Financial expenses ratio |\n| **Profit before tax** | **120** | **13.33** | Pre-tax profit ratio |\n| Less: Tax at 25% | 30 | 3.33 | |\n| **Profit after tax** | **90** | **10.00** | Net profit ratio |\n\n::: steps\n1. **Gross profit ratio** = gross profit ÷ sales × 100 = **40%**: the percentage of each sale in rupees remaining after payment for the goods sold. It depends on the relationship between price, volume and costs; a high gross profit margin is a favourable sign of good management (SM 3.20).\n2. **Operating profit ratio** = operating profit ÷ sales × 100 = **14.67%**, where operating profit = sales − COGS − operating expenses, often referred to as EBIT. It is the percentage of each sale that remains after all costs and expenses except interest and taxes, followed closely by analysts because it focuses on operating results (SM 3.20–3.21).\n3. **Expenses ratios**: COGS ratio 60%; **operating expenses ratio** = (administrative + selling and distribution expenses) ÷ sales = 228 ÷ 900 = 25.33%; **operating ratio** = (COGS + operating expenses) ÷ sales = 768 ÷ 900 = **85.33%**, the cost of earning each rupee of sales; **financial expenses ratio** = 12 ÷ 900 = 1.33% (financial expenses exclude taxes, loss due to theft, goods destroyed by fire and the like). Administration and selling and distribution expense ratios are calculated the same way (SM 3.21).\n4. **Net profit ratio** = net profit (EAT) ÷ sales × 100 = **10%**; the **pre-tax profit ratio** = EBT ÷ sales × 100 = 13.33%. It finds the proportion of revenue that finds its way into profits after meeting all expenses (SM 3.20).\n:::\n\nSince operating profit is what sales leave after COGS and operating expenses, **operating ratio + operating profit ratio = 100%**: 85.33% + 14.67%. ICAI used exactly this in May 2024 Q1(a), where an operating ratio of 85% gave an operating profit ratio of 15%.\n\n### Returns: profit over the money invested\n\nROI is \"the most important ratio of all\": the percentage return on funds invested in the business, which tells the owner whether all the effort put into the business has been worthwhile. It splits into two ratios you already know (SM 3.22):\n\n$$\\text{ROI} = \\frac{\\text{Return}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times \\frac{\\text{Sales}}{\\text{Investment}} \\qquad = \\text{Profitability ratio} \\times \\text{Investment turnover}$$\n\nSo ROI can be improved by improving the margin, the turnover, or both. \"Investment\" may be total assets or net assets; funds employed in net assets are called **capital employed**, which is net worth plus debt; and the return may be measured in several ways according to the requirement and the information available (SM 3.23). Tapri (₹ crore): total assets 505; capital employed 435 (equity 300 + preference 25 + debt 110); EBIT 132; tax rate 25%.\n\n::: diagram The return on investment family\n{\"type\": \"tree\", \"root\": {\"label\": \"Return on\\ninvestment\", \"children\": [\n {\"label\": \"Return on\\nassets (ROA)\", \"color\": \"a\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"ROTA\"}, {\"label\": \"RONA\"}]},\n {\"label\": \"Return on capital\\nemployed (ROCE)\", \"color\": \"b\"},\n {\"label\": \"Return on\\nequity (ROE)\", \"color\": \"c\"}\n ]},\n \"caption\": \"ICAI's chart (SM 3.22): three broad categories of ROI, by the investment in the denominator; ROTA (on total assets) and RONA (on net assets) are the ROA measured on EBIT(1 − t).\"}\n:::\n\n| Ratio | Return ÷ investment | Tapri |\n|---|---|---:|\n| ROA (on profit after tax) | 90 ÷ 505 | 17.82% |\n| ROA (with interest added back) | (90 + 12) ÷ 505 | 20.20% |\n| Return on total assets, ROTA | 132 × (1 − 0.25) ÷ 505 | 19.60% |\n| Return on net assets, RONA | 132 × (1 − 0.25) ÷ 435 | 22.76% |\n| ROCE, pre-tax | 132 ÷ 435 | 30.34% |\n| ROCE, post-tax | 99 ÷ 435 | 22.76% |\n| ROE | (90 − 2) ÷ 300 | 29.33% |\n\nCheck the split for ROCE: operating profit ratio 14.67% × capital turnover 2.069 (900 ÷ 435) = 30.34%. Tapri's ROCE comfortably exceeds the 10–12% it pays on debt, and ICAI's rule is that ==ROCE should always be higher than the rate at which the company borrows== (SM 3.24).\n\n**Return on equity** = (profit after tax − preference dividend) ÷ net worth (equity shareholders' funds) × 100. It measures how profitably the owners' funds have been utilised, and it is one of the most important indicators of a firm's profitability and potential growth. But \"two companies can have the same return on equity, yet one can be a much better business\" (SM 3.24–3.25), which is where DuPont comes in.\n\n::: lab ratios {\"mode\":\"dash\",\"group\":\"prof\"}\nStep through the profitability group: every margin and return with its formula, its working and a one-line reading.\n:::\n\n### DuPont: one return, three engines\n\nA finance executive at E.I. Du Pont de Nemours and Co. created the DuPont system of financial analysis in 1919; it is still used around the world as the basis of the components that make up return on equity (SM 3.25):\n\n$$\\text{ROE} = \\frac{\\text{Net income}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times \\frac{\\text{Sales}}{\\text{Assets}} \\qquad \\times \\frac{\\text{Assets}}{\\text{Shareholders’ equity}}$$\n\nTapri Premix Ltd (Tapri's packaged-chai subsidiary, with no preference shares): sales ₹200 crore, profit after tax ₹8 crore, total assets ₹80 crore, financed by ₹50 crore of equity and ₹30 crore of debt and current liabilities.\n\n::: diagram Tapri Premix: ROE in three parts\n{\"type\": \"tree\", \"gapX\": 30, \"root\": {\"label\": \"ROE\\n16%\", \"color\": \"a\", \"children\": [\n {\"label\": \"Net profit\\nmargin 4%\", \"note\": \"PAT ÷ sales\", \"color\": \"b\"},\n {\"label\": \"Asset\\nturnover 2.5\", \"note\": \"sales ÷ assets\", \"color\": \"c\"},\n {\"label\": \"Equity\\nmultiplier 1.6\", \"note\": \"assets ÷ equity\", \"color\": \"d\"}\n ]},\n \"caption\": \"4% × 2.5 × 1.6 = 16%. Each child is one engine of the return: margin, turnover and leverage.\"}\n:::\n\n::: steps\n1. **Net profit margin** = 8 ÷ 200 = 4%: the after-tax profit generated for each rupee of revenue. It varies across industries, so compare with competitors; it is a safety cushion, and the lower it is, the less room for error: a business with a 1% margin has no room for flawed execution (SM 3.25–3.26).\n2. **Asset turnover** (investment or capital turnover) = 200 ÷ 80 = 2.5: how effectively the company converts its assets into sales. It tends to be inversely related to the net profit margin, which lets an investor compare a low-profit, high-volume model with a high-profit, low-volume one (SM 3.26).\n3. **Equity multiplier** = 80 ÷ 50 = 1.6: a measure of financial leverage. A company with terrible sales and margins can take on excessive debt and artificially increase its ROE; the multiplier shows what portion of ROE is the result of debt (SM 3.26).\n:::\n\nLeave out the multiplier to see what the company would earn if it were completely debt-free, as the Study Material does in its Example 1 (SM 3.27): 4% × 2.5 = 10%. So 10 points of Tapri Premix's 16% come from margins and sales, and the other 6 points from the debt at work in the business. The Study Material's conclusion: of two companies with the same ROE at a comparable valuation, the one whose return arises more from internally generated sales is the more attractive.\n\n::: diagram Margin against turnover\n{\"type\": \"plot\", \"h\": 310, \"x\": [0, 3], \"y\": [0, 15], \"xlabel\": \"asset turnover (times)\", \"ylabel\": \"net profit margin (%)\",\n \"xticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [0.5, \"0.5\"], [1, \"1.0\"], [1.5, \"1.5\"], [2, \"2.0\"], [2.5, \"2.5\"], [3, \"3.0\"]],\n \"yticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [5, \"5\"], [10, \"10\"], [15, \"15\"]],\n \"clip\": true, \"legend\": \"top-right\",\n \"series\": [\n  {\"label\": \"margin × turnover = 10%\", \"fn\": \"10/x\", \"color\": \"a\"}\n ],\n \"points\": [{\"x\": 1, \"y\": 10, \"label\": \"tea boutiques\", \"pos\": \"right\"}, {\"x\": 2.5, \"y\": 4, \"label\": \"Tapri Premix\", \"pos\": \"above\"}],\n \"caption\": \"Every point on the blue curve earns the same 10% before leverage. Tapri Premix (4% margin, turnover 2.5: low profit, high volume) and Tapri's Reserve tea boutiques (10% margin, turnover 1.0: high profit, low volume) reach it by opposite models; a point below the curve earns less, a point above it more.\"}\n:::\n\n::: lab ratios {\"mode\":\"dupont\"}\nGrow the DuPont tree yourself, branch by branch, and see how much of the ROE comes from borrowing.\n:::\n\n### Per share, and against the share price\n\nTapri has 4 crore equity shares of ₹10 and paid a dividend of ₹5.50 a share; the market price is ₹330; net worth is ₹300 crore.\n\n::: steps\n1. **Earnings per share** = net profit available to equity shareholders ÷ number of equity shares outstanding = (90 − 2) ÷ 4 = **₹22**. It is the profitability of the firm from the ordinary shareholder's point of view (SM 3.27).\n2. **Dividend per share** = total dividend paid to equity shareholders ÷ number of equity shares = **₹5.50**: how much of the profit is distributed per share (SM 3.27). **Dividend pay-out ratio** = DPS ÷ EPS = 5.50 ÷ 22 = **25%**: the extent to which earnings are paid out and, by difference, retained (SM 3.28).\n3. **Price-earnings ratio** = market price per share ÷ EPS = 330 ÷ 22 = **15 times**. It indicates the expectation of equity investors about the firm's earnings, and is taken as a summary measure of growth potential, risk characteristics, shareholders' orientation, corporate image and degree of liquidity. It indicates the payback period to investors; a higher P/E could mean the share is over-valued or that investors expect high growth (SM 3.28).\n4. **Dividend yield** = DPS ÷ MPS × 100 = 5.50 ÷ 330 = **1.67%**; **earnings yield** (earnings-price ratio) = EPS ÷ MPS × 100 = **6.67%**, the reciprocal of the P/E. Dividend (%) shows the return on the paid-up value of shares; yield (%) is the indicator of true return, with share capital taken at its market value (SM 3.28–3.29).\n5. **Market value to book value per share** = 330 ÷ (300 ÷ 4) = 330 ÷ 75 = **4.4**: the market response to the shareholders' investment; the higher, the better the shareholders' position in terms of return and capital gains (SM 3.29).\n6. **Q ratio** (proposed by James Tobin) = market value of equity and liabilities ÷ estimated replacement cost of assets. It relates market valuation to intrinsic value: equilibrium is 1; below 1 the stock could be undervalued, above 1 overvalued (SM 3.29).\n:::\n\n::: lab ratios {\"mode\":\"dash\",\"group\":\"mkt\"}\nStep through the market group: EPS, dividends per share, payout, yields and the P/E ratio, each with its working.\n:::\n\n## Formats and formulas\n\n*Reproduce these from memory. Every \"× 100\" ratio is a percentage; the others are in times or rupees.*\n\n::: formula Margins on sales\n$$\\text{Gross profit ratio} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{Gross profit}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{Net profit ratio} = \\frac{\\text{EAT}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{Pre-tax profit ratio} = \\frac{\\text{EBT}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{Operating profit ratio} = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100$$\nOperating profit = sales − COGS − operating expenses (SM 3.20–3.21).\n:::\n\n::: formula Expenses ratios\n$$\\text{COGS ratio} = \\frac{\\text{COGS}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{Operating expenses ratio} \\qquad = \\frac{A + S}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{Operating ratio} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{COGS} + \\text{Operating expenses}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{Financial expenses ratio} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{Financial expenses}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100$$\nA = administrative expenses; S = selling and distribution overheads. Financial expenses exclude taxes, loss due to theft, goods destroyed by fire etc. (SM 3.21).\n:::\n\n::: formula Return on investment\n$$\\text{ROI} = \\frac{\\text{Return}}{\\text{Investment}} \\times 100 \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{Return}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times \\frac{\\text{Sales}}{\\text{Investment}}$$\n(SM 3.22)\n:::\n\n::: formula Return on assets\n$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{\\text{PAT}}{\\text{Average total assets}} \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\frac{\\text{PAT} + \\text{Interest}}{\\text{Average total assets}}$$\n$$\\text{ROTA} = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}(1 - t)}{\\text{Average total assets}} \\qquad \\text{RONA} = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}(1 - t)}{\\text{Average net assets}}$$\nPAT = net profit after taxes; t = tax rate. Average tangible assets or average fixed assets may replace total assets in the first two; total assets are sometimes used instead of average assets (SM 3.23).\n:::\n\n::: formula Return on capital employed\n$$\\text{ROCE (pre-tax)} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{Capital employed}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{ROCE (post-tax)} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}(1 - t)}{\\text{Capital employed}} \\times 100$$\nSometimes (PAT + interest) ÷ capital employed. Capital employed = total assets − current liabilities = fixed assets + working capital = equity + long-term debt; include intangibles (goodwill, patents, trade marks), exclude fictitious assets (deferred expenses); use average capital employed if available (SM 3.24, 3.30).\n:::\n\n::: formula Return on equity and the DuPont model\n$$\\text{ROE} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{PAT} - \\text{Preference dividend}}{\\text{Equity shareholders funds}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net profit margin} \\qquad \\times \\text{Asset turnover} \\qquad \\times \\text{Equity multiplier}$$\nNet profit margin = net income ÷ sales; asset (investment, capital) turnover = sales ÷ investment, assets or capital; equity multiplier = investment, assets or capital ÷ shareholders' equity (SM 3.25–3.26).\n:::\n\n::: formula Owner's ratios\n$$\\text{EPS} = \\frac{\\text{Equity earnings}}{\\text{No. of equity shares}} \\qquad \\text{DPS} = \\frac{\\text{Equity dividend}}{\\text{No. of equity shares}}$$\n$$\\text{Dividend pay-out ratio} = \\frac{\\text{DPS}}{\\text{EPS}}$$\nEquity earnings = net profit available to equity shareholders; equity dividend = total dividend paid to equity shareholders; both are divided by the number of equity shares outstanding. In the absence of preference dividend, PAT is the earnings available to equity shareholders (SM 3.27–3.28, 3.30).\n:::\n\n::: formula Market ratios\n$$\\text{P/E ratio} = \\frac{\\text{MPS}}{\\text{EPS}} \\qquad \\text{Earnings yield} = \\frac{\\text{EPS}}{\\text{MPS}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{Dividend yield} = \\frac{\\text{DPS}}{\\text{MPS}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\frac{\\text{Dividend} \\pm \\text{Price change}}{\\text{Initial share price}} \\times 100$$\n$$\\text{MV/BV} = \\frac{\\text{Share price}}{\\text{Book value per share}} \\qquad \\text{Q ratio} \\qquad = \\frac{\\text{Market value of a company}}{\\text{Replacement cost of assets}}$$\nShare price = the average or the closing share price; book value per share = net worth ÷ number of equity shares; price change = the change in share price over the period. The Q ratio is also written as market value of equity and liabilities ÷ estimated replacement cost of assets (SM 3.28–3.29).\n:::\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Tapri Cold Chain: sales up 50%, return down\nTapri Cold Chain Pvt Ltd, the refrigerated-transport subsidiary, expanded its fleet in 2025-26 with ₹15 crore of new 10% debentures issued on 1 April 2025. Kesar calls it \"our best year yet: sales are up 50%\". Its summarised figures (₹ crore; tax rate 25%; there are no preference shares):\n\n| Particulars | 2024-25 | 2025-26 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sales | 40.00 | 60.00 |\n| Direct costs | 28.00 | 45.00 |\n| Operating expenses | 6.00 | 7.50 |\n\n| Balance sheet at 31 March | 2025 | 2026 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Equity share capital (₹10 shares) | 10.00 | 10.00 |\n| Reserves and surplus | 15.00 | 17.50 |\n| 10% Debentures | 10.00 | 25.00 |\n| Current liabilities | 5.00 | 7.50 |\n| **Total equity and liabilities** | **40.00** | **60.00** |\n| Fixed assets (net) | 30.00 | 46.00 |\n| Current assets | 10.00 | 14.00 |\n| **Total assets** | **40.00** | **60.00** |\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Prepare the statement of profit for both years, and calculate the gross profit ratio, operating ratio, operating profit ratio and net profit ratio.\n\n(ii) Calculate the pre-tax return on capital employed and the return on equity for both years, using closing balances.\n\n(iii) Split each year's ROE with the DuPont model, and explain what changed.\n\n(iv) Calculate the 2025-26 ROE had the extra ₹15 crore been raised by issuing equity shares instead of debentures, and comment on the use of debt.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Statement of profit and ratios on sales**\n\n| Particulars (₹ crore) | 2024-25 | 2025-26 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sales | 40.00 | 60.00 |\n| Less: Direct costs | 28.00 | 45.00 |\n| **Gross profit** | **12.00** | **15.00** |\n| Less: Operating expenses | 6.00 | 7.50 |\n| **EBIT** | **6.00** | **7.50** |\n| Less: Interest at 10% on debentures | 1.00 | 2.50 |\n| **Profit before tax** | **5.00** | **5.00** |\n| Less: Tax at 25% | 1.25 | 1.25 |\n| **Profit after tax** | **3.75** | **3.75** |\n\n| Ratio | 2024-25 | 2025-26 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Gross profit ratio | 30.00% | 25.00% |\n| Operating ratio | 85.00% | 87.50% |\n| Operating profit ratio | 15.00% | 12.50% |\n| Net profit ratio | 9.38% | 6.25% |\n\nGross profit ratio = gross profit ÷ sales; operating ratio = (direct costs + operating expenses) ÷ sales; operating profit ratio = EBIT ÷ sales; net profit ratio = PAT ÷ sales; all × 100. Check: 85% + 15% = 100% and 87.5% + 12.5% = 100%.\n\n**(ii) Returns**\n\nCapital employed = total assets − current liabilities = 40 − 5 = ₹35 crore (2025) and 60 − 7.50 = ₹52.50 crore (2026). Equity shareholders' funds = share capital + reserves = ₹25 crore and ₹27.50 crore.\n\n| Ratio | 2024-25 | 2025-26 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ROCE, pre-tax: EBIT ÷ capital employed | 6 ÷ 35 = 17.14% | 7.50 ÷ 52.50 = 14.29% |\n| ROE: PAT ÷ equity funds | 3.75 ÷ 25 = 15.00% | 3.75 ÷ 27.50 = 13.64% |\n\n**(iii) DuPont**\n\n| Component | 2024-25 | 2025-26 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Net profit margin (PAT ÷ sales) | 9.375% | 6.25% |\n| Asset turnover (sales ÷ total assets) | 1.00 | 1.00 |\n| Equity multiplier (total assets ÷ equity) | 40 ÷ 25 = 1.60 | 60 ÷ 27.50 = 2.18 |\n| **ROE** | **15.00%** | **13.64%** |\n\nROE fell from 15% to 13.64% although sales rose 50%. Asset turnover did not change: the new fleet produced sales in proportion. The net profit margin fell from 9.375% to 6.25%, for two reasons: direct costs rose from 70% to 75% of sales, so the operating profit ratio fell from 15% to 12.5%; and interest rose from 2.5% to 4.17% of sales on the new debentures. The higher equity multiplier (1.60 to 2.18) made up for only part of the fall in margin.\n\n**(iv) Had the ₹15 crore been raised as equity**\n\nInterest stays at ₹1 crore (10% of ₹10 crore); PBT = 7.50 − 1.00 = ₹6.50 crore; PAT = ₹4.875 crore; equity = 27.50 + 15 = ₹42.50 crore; ROE = 4.875 ÷ 42.50 = **11.47%**.\n\n**Comment.** Debt financing gave the equity shareholders 13.64% instead of 11.47%, because the capital employed still earns a pre-tax 14.29%, more than the 10% paid on the debentures. ROE fell because operations became less profitable, not because of the debt. The cushion is thinner, though: ROCE has come down from 17.14% to 14.29%, and if it falls below the 10% rate of borrowing, the debentures will start to reduce ROE (see [[fm.lev.leverage|trading on equity]]). Kesar's \"best year\" is a year of growth with a lower return on equity.\n:::\n::::\n\n:::: example Cafés against BrewBucks: whose 12.5% is better?\nKesar wants to tell the board that Tapri Kulhad Cafés Ltd \"earns as well as BrewBucks India\". Dev extracts (₹ crore):\n\n| Particulars | Tapri Kulhad Cafés | BrewBucks India |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sales | 50.00 | 80.00 |\n| Profit after tax | 1.875 | 8.00 |\n| Total assets | 40.00 | 80.00 |\n| Shareholders' equity | 15.00 | 64.00 |\n\nNeither company has preference shares.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Calculate each company's ROE using the DuPont model.\n\n(ii) Calculate the ROE each would earn without the equity multiplier, and the part of ROE that is due to leverage.\n\n(iii) Advise Kesar on his claim.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) DuPont**\n\n| Component | Tapri Kulhad Cafés | BrewBucks India |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Net profit margin (PAT ÷ sales) | 1.875 ÷ 50 = 3.75% | 8 ÷ 80 = 10.00% |\n| Asset turnover (sales ÷ assets) | 50 ÷ 40 = 1.25 | 80 ÷ 80 = 1.00 |\n| Equity multiplier (assets ÷ equity) | 40 ÷ 15 = 2.667 | 80 ÷ 64 = 1.25 |\n| **ROE** | **12.50%** | **12.50%** |\n\n**(ii) Without the multiplier**\n\n| Particulars | Tapri Kulhad Cafés | BrewBucks India |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Margin × turnover | 3.75% × 1.25 = 4.69% | 10% × 1.00 = 10.00% |\n| Part of ROE due to leverage | 12.50 − 4.69 = 7.81 points | 12.50 − 10.00 = 2.50 points |\n\n**(iii) Advice**\n\nThe two returns on equity are equal, but they are earned differently. BrewBucks India earns 10% on its sales and assets before leverage, and only 2.5 points of its ROE come from debt. The cafés earn 4.69% from operations and 7.81 points, more than half their ROE, from debt: their margin is thin (3.75%), so there is little room for error, and each ₹1 of equity carries ₹2.67 of assets. The claim is not correct: on equal ROE, the business whose return arises from its own margins and sales is the more attractive. The cafés should work on their margin, or on the sales produced by each rupee of assets, rather than on further borrowing.\n:::\n::::\n\n:::: example Chambal Snacks Ltd: the share price talks back\nChambal Snacks Ltd, a listed snack maker that competes with Tapri's samosas, has 50 lakh equity shares of ₹10 and 9% preference share capital of ₹10 crore. Its share trades at ₹240, at a P/E of 12 times and 1.6 times its book value per share. It pays out 40% of its earnings to equity shareholders. A year ago the share was ₹200.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Calculate the EPS, DPS, dividend yield and earnings yield.\n\n(ii) Calculate the book value per share, net worth and return on equity.\n\n(iii) Calculate the profit after tax, the equity dividend coverage and the total dividend coverage (profit after tax ÷ all dividends).\n\n(iv) Calculate the yield including the change in share price over the year.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Per share and yields**\n\n- EPS = MPS ÷ P/E = 240 ÷ 12 = **₹20**\n- DPS = 40% × ₹20 = **₹8**\n- Dividend yield = 8 ÷ 240 × 100 = **3.33%**\n- Earnings yield = 20 ÷ 240 × 100 = **8.33%** (= 1 ÷ 12)\n\n**(ii) Book value and ROE**\n\n- Book value per share = MPS ÷ (MV/BV) = 240 ÷ 1.6 = **₹150**\n- Net worth (equity shareholders' funds) = ₹150 × 50 lakh shares = **₹75 crore**\n- Earnings for equity = ₹20 × 50 lakh = ₹10 crore; ROE = 10 ÷ 75 = **13.33%** (also EPS ÷ book value per share)\n\n**(iii) Profit and dividend cover**\n\n- Preference dividend = 9% × ₹10 crore = ₹0.90 crore; PAT = 10 + 0.90 = **₹10.90 crore**\n- Equity dividend = ₹8 × 50 lakh = ₹4 crore; equity dividend coverage = (10.90 − 0.90) ÷ 4 = **2.5 times**\n- Total dividend coverage = 10.90 ÷ (4 + 0.90) = **2.22 times**\n\n**(iv) Yield with the price change**\n\n$$\\text{Yield} = \\frac{\\text{Dividend} + \\text{Change in price}}{\\text{Initial price}} \\times 100 \\qquad = \\frac{8 + (240 - 200)}{200} \\times 100 \\qquad = 24\\%$$\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### The variants, and how to choose\n\n- **Which return, which investment.** The Study Material keeps saying that returns and investments \"may vary as per the requirement and availability of information\" (SM 3.23). Its notes: EBIT = PBIT, EAT = PAT, EBT = PBT; numerator in correspondence with the denominator; average capital employed if available; assumptions stated if needed (SM 3.30). A return after interest (PAT) belongs over equity; a return before interest (EBIT, or PAT + interest, or EBIT(1 − t)) belongs over assets or capital employed, which lenders also finance.\n- **ROE for all shareholders.** If the return to equity and preference shareholders together is wanted, profit after tax before preference dividend is divided by total shareholders' funds including preference capital (SM 3.25).\n- **Two ROAs.** \"Here, net profit is exclusive of interest\": because lenders also finance assets, ROA can add interest back, or use EBIT(1 − t) (SM 3.23). September 2025 Q1(b) took ROA as EBIT(1 − t) ÷ total assets and accepted a net profit margin either on PAT or on EBIT(1 − t).\n\n### Coverage of dividends\n\nThe preference and equity dividend coverage ratios are in [[fm.ratio.liquidity|the coverage ratios]]. May 2026 Q1(c) used a **total dividend coverage ratio** = PAT ÷ (equity dividend + preference dividend) and asked for the preference dividend rate from it, by solving (6,00,000 + P) ÷ (1,92,000 + P) = 2.863 for P.\n\n### Extended DuPont\n\nRTP January 2026 MCQ 3 wrote ROE = NPM × TATR × x/y and asked for x/y: total assets ÷ equity, the equity multiplier. When there are preference shares, NPM × TAT × EM gives PAT ÷ equity, which is ROE only after the preference dividend is deducted from PAT; work the DuPont split on companies without preference capital, or say what you did.\n\n### Where these ratios lead\n\nThe P/E and the earnings yield reappear in valuing shares and the cost of equity ([[fm.coc.equity|cost of equity]]); the pay-out ratio and retention drive the dividend models ([[fm.div.models|Walter and Gordon]]); trading on equity, the reason the equity multiplier can raise ROE, is in [[fm.lev.leverage|financial leverage]].\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam Where these ratios appeared, May 2024 to September 2026\nIn the ICAI documents read for this lesson:\n\n- **Descriptive, 5 marks.** September 2025 Q1(b): PAT, net profit margin, return on assets (after tax), asset turnover and ROE from total assets, a debt share, direct costs and operating expenses (1 mark each). May 2026 Q1(c): total equity dividend, EPS, rate of preference dividend and PAT from a total dividend coverage ratio, a dividend yield, the market price and a P/E. May 2024 Q1(a): gross profit ratio from an operating ratio and a ratio of operating expenses to COGS, with interest coverage from a return on shareholders' fund.\n- **MCQs.** September 2024 MCQ 8 (ROE by DuPont from total assets, equity, net profit margin and asset turnover); September 2026 MCQ 8 (total asset turnover from a net profit margin and a return on assets); January 2025 MCQ 2 (market price from EPS and P/E, in an EBIT-EPS case); RTP January 2026 MCQ 3 (the third term of the extended DuPont model).\n- **RTPs and MTPs.** RTP January 2025 Q4: EPS and P/E inside a larger ratio question; RTP January 2026 Q5: EPS, market value to book value and P/E, each named by what it measures (\"indicates market response of the equity shareholders' investment\"). MTP Series II for May 2026 Q1(c): inventory turnover, financial leverage, ROCE, ROE and collection period from published statements. MTP Series II for September 2026: net profit ratio and return on total assets against industry averages.\n:::\n\n::: exam The traps ICAI set\n- EPS and ROE use profit **after** preference dividend; P/E then follows from EPS.\n- Operating ratio and operating profit ratio add up to 100% (May 2024 Q1(a)).\n- \"Return on assets (after tax)\" in September 2025 Q1(b) meant EBIT(1 − t) ÷ assets; name your basis.\n- In DuPont MCQs a distractor may invert the multiplier (equity ÷ assets): 12.5% × 1.5 × 0.625 = 11.72% is one of the wrong options in September 2024 MCQ 8, whose answer is 12.5% × 1.5 × 1.6 = 30%.\n:::\n\n::: trick Three shortcuts from ICAI's identities\nROE = ROA (on PAT) × equity multiplier; EPS ÷ book value per share = ROE; earnings yield = 1 ÷ P/E. Conditions: no preference capital or dividend (otherwise use profit after preference dividend and equity shareholders' funds throughout), and the same profit figure and the same balance sheet date on both sides.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n### \"Discuss the DuPont model\" (SM 3.65, theory questions 4 and 6)\n\n1. Origin: created in 1919 by a finance executive at E.I. Du Pont de Nemours and Co.; used worldwide as the basis of the components of ROE.\n2. The model: ROE = net profit margin × asset (investment) turnover × equity multiplier.\n3. Each component, its formula and meaning: net profit margin (profit for each rupee of revenue; a safety cushion); asset turnover (how effectively assets are converted into sales; inversely related to margin); equity multiplier (financial leverage; shows what portion of ROE results from debt).\n4. Use: by examining each input, the sources of a company's ROE can be discovered and compared with competitors; a high ROE from leverage is less attractive than one from margins and turnover.\n5. A two-line numerical illustration.\n\n### \"Discuss any three ratios computed for investment analysis\" (SM 3.65, theory question 1)\n\nThe Study Material's answer key points to paragraph 3.4.2, the return on investment ratios. For each of ROA, ROCE and ROE: definition, formula with the components named, interpretation (ROCE above the borrowing rate; ROE as an indicator of profitability and potential growth), and who uses it.\n\n### Three answers to \"calculate the return on equity\"\n\n::: cols\n**Loses marks**\n\n\"ROE = 3.75 ÷ 60 = 6.25%.\"\n\nTotal assets in the denominator: that is a return on assets.\n---\n**Passes**\n\n\"ROE = 3.75 ÷ 27.50 = 13.64%.\"\n\nCorrect, but the equity figure is unexplained.\n---\n**Full marks**\n\nWorking note: equity shareholders' funds = share capital ₹10 crore + reserves ₹17.50 crore = ₹27.50 crore; PAT from the statement of profit; ROE = (PAT − preference dividend) ÷ equity shareholders' funds × 100 = 13.64%; DuPont check if asked.\n:::\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: the preference dividend that went to the equity shareholders\nEPS taken as PAT ÷ shares: Chambal's ₹10.90 crore ÷ 50 lakh = ₹21.80 instead of ₹20, and every ratio built on it (P/E, yields, ROE) goes wrong. **Fix:** deduct the preference dividend first; only what is left is available to equity shareholders.\n:::\n\n::: mistake The return that did not match its investment\nEBIT over equity, or PAT over total assets labelled \"ROCE\". **Fix:** returns before interest (EBIT, EBIT(1 − t), PAT + interest) go over assets or capital employed; returns after interest and preference dividend go over equity shareholders' funds.\n:::\n\n::: mistake DuPont with two engines\nMultiplying margin and turnover and calling it ROE (the cafés' 4.69%). **Fix:** that product is a return on assets; ROE needs the equity multiplier too.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Fictitious assets in capital employed\nAdding deferred expenses or accumulated losses to capital employed lowers ROCE without a single rupee more invested. **Fix:** include intangibles such as goodwill, patents and trade marks; exclude fictitious assets (SM 3.24).\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: Tapri's kiosk company has sales of ₹75 crore, total assets of ₹50 crore and shareholders' equity of ₹20 crore, and no preference shares. Its ROE is 18%. What is its net profit margin, in per cent?\n= 4.79..4.81\n> Asset turnover = 75 ÷ 50 = 1.5; equity multiplier = 50 ÷ 20 = 2.5; ROE = NPM × 1.5 × 2.5, so NPM = 18% ÷ 3.75 = 4.8%. Check: PAT = 18% × ₹20 crore = ₹3.6 crore, and 3.6 ÷ 75 = 4.8%.\n\nQ: Case scenario. To lift Tapri Kulhad Cafés' ROE before an investor meeting, Kesar proposes to borrow ₹5 crore and buy back shares of the same amount, with sales, operating profit and the asset base unchanged. Dev warns that this changes only one part of the DuPont equation directly. Which part?\n- [ ] Net profit margin, because interest is an operating expense\n- [ ] Asset turnover, because borrowing adds to the assets\n- [x] Equity multiplier, because the same assets are financed by less equity\n- [ ] None, because a buyback does not affect ROE\n> Assets are unchanged and equity falls, so assets ÷ equity rises. The margin also falls indirectly (more interest, lower PAT), but the direct lever is the multiplier: ROE rises only while the return on assets exceeds the cost of the new debt.\n\nQ: According to ICAI, a Q ratio of more than 1 could mean that the stock is:\n- [ ] Undervalued\n- [x] Overvalued\n- [ ] In equilibrium\n- [ ] Not quoted\n> Q = market value of equity and liabilities ÷ replacement cost of assets. Equilibrium is 1; below 1 the stock could be undervalued, above 1 overvalued (SM 3.29).\n\nQ: A company's profit before tax is ₹80 lakh and the tax rate 25%. It has 10% preference share capital of ₹1 crore and 8 lakh equity shares. What is its EPS?\n- [ ] ₹7.50\n- [ ] ₹6.56\n- [x] ₹6.25\n- [ ] ₹8.75\n> PAT = 80 × 75% = ₹60 lakh; preference dividend = ₹10 lakh, paid out of profit after tax; earnings for equity = ₹50 lakh; EPS = 50 ÷ 8 = ₹6.25. ₹7.50 forgets the preference dividend; ₹6.56 deducts it before tax, as if it were interest ((80 − 10) × 75% ÷ 8); ₹8.75 uses profit before tax.\n\nQ: A firm's cost of goods sold is 62% of sales and its operating expenses 23% of sales. Which statements are correct? Select all that apply.\n- [x] Its operating ratio is 85%\n- [x] Its operating profit ratio is 15%\n- [ ] Its gross profit ratio is 15%\n- [x] Its gross profit ratio is 38%\n> Operating ratio = 62% + 23% = 85%; operating profit ratio = 100% − 85% = 15%; gross profit ratio = 100% − 62% = 38%.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Gross profit, operating profit (EBIT) and net profit ratios = that profit ÷ sales × 100; operating ratio = (COGS + operating expenses) ÷ sales, and operating ratio + operating profit ratio = 100%.\n- ROI = margin × investment turnover; ROA = PAT (or PAT + interest, or EBIT(1 − t)) ÷ average assets.\n- ROCE = EBIT (pre-tax) or EBIT(1 − t) (post-tax) ÷ capital employed; it should exceed the borrowing rate; intangibles in, fictitious assets out.\n- ROE = (PAT − preference dividend) ÷ equity shareholders' funds.\n- DuPont: ROE = net profit margin × asset turnover × equity multiplier (assets ÷ equity); the multiplier shows the part of ROE that comes from debt.\n- EPS = earnings for equity ÷ equity shares; DPS; pay-out = DPS ÷ EPS.\n- P/E = MPS ÷ EPS; dividend yield = DPS ÷ MPS (or dividend ± price change ÷ initial price); earnings yield = EPS ÷ MPS; MV/BV; Q ratio = market value ÷ replacement cost (1 = equilibrium).\n- Same ROE, different businesses: ask which engine produced it. (Borrowed money sends a bill every month.)"});
